ight-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/state.json" "file_sha256" "388a8e5b40a8eee77b9b6e5c872fbe9cc20cdf78f22584b7cc618c32262b3c54"} {"question" "q-141016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "085e0c4962b08f5af8d57216626efe3abb2a896cc7a82086a8c07947eb358e2e"} {"question" "q-141016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/state.json" "file_sha256" "388a8e5b40a8eee77b9b6e5c872fbe9cc20cdf78f22584b7cc618c32262b3c54"} {"question" "q-141016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/state.json" "file_sha256" "388a8e5b40a8eee77b9b6e5c872fbe9cc20cdf78f22584b7cc618c32262b3c54"} {"question" "q-141016" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/state.json" "file_sha256" "388a8e5b40a8eee77b9b6e5c872fbe9cc20cdf78f22584b7cc618c32262b3c54"} {"question" "q-141014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x<0 \\\\ -1, & x=0 \\\\ x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/state.json" "file_sha256" "388a8e5b40a8eee77b9b6e5c872fbe9cc20cdf78f22584b7cc618c32262b3c54"} {"question" "q-141014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & =\\frac{1}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=0$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne g(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/state.json" "file_sha256" "388a8e5b40a8eee77b9b6e5c872fbe9cc20cdf78f22584b7cc618c32262b3c54"} {"question" "q-141014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-cap